1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:03,259
Okay, so now the project is on our VPS. How

2
00:00:03,709 --> 00:00:04,399
do we continue now?

3
00:00:05,330 --> 00:00:08,092
Well, as a next step, we have to make sure

4
00:00:08,204 --> 00:00:10,195
that we set up our environment variables

5
00:00:10,388 --> 00:00:11,930
because we'll need some, as I mentioned.

6
00:00:12,315 --> 00:00:15,237
We'll need to make sure that we do let

7
00:00:15,767 --> 00:00:18,304
Docker and Traefik in the end know about

8
00:00:18,336 --> 00:00:20,311
which domain we're about to use so that it

9
00:00:20,343 --> 00:00:23,233
can request the right certificate and that

10
00:00:23,233 --> 00:00:25,048
we provide a valid email address.

11
00:00:26,207 --> 00:00:28,546
To do that, we can follow the instructions I

12
00:00:28,546 --> 00:00:30,384
shared in this deployment.md file.

13
00:00:30,950 --> 00:00:32,126
We wanna cd

14
00:00:32,738 --> 00:00:34,365
into our project

15
00:00:35,332 --> 00:00:37,958
and then we wanna copy the.env.example

16
00:00:37,991 --> 00:00:39,070
file, which you find in there.

17
00:00:41,647 --> 00:00:44,083
And now you wanna edit this.env file

18
00:00:44,083 --> 00:00:45,987
because we're loading environment variables

19
00:00:46,083 --> 00:00:46,793
from that file.

20
00:00:47,471 --> 00:00:48,826
We can do this with nano,

21
00:00:49,390 --> 00:00:50,262
nano.env,

22
00:00:50,858 --> 00:00:52,912
and you see It already has the environment

23
00:00:52,912 --> 00:00:54,888
variables filled in, but you need to bring

24
00:00:55,097 --> 00:00:56,543
your concrete values here.

25
00:00:57,202 --> 00:00:58,761
So as mentioned, if you wanna follow along

26
00:00:58,857 --> 00:00:59,837
here, you'll need a domain.

27
00:01:00,496 --> 00:01:03,115
So I'll buy a new domain here for this

28
00:01:03,115 --> 00:01:04,995
course. Obviously you don't need to do that.

29
00:01:05,301 --> 00:01:07,245
You can also use one you got lying around.

30
00:01:07,711 --> 00:01:10,555
vps-course is available, so I'll purchase

31
00:01:10,603 --> 00:01:13,207
that. And now that I did purchase it, I

32
00:01:13,319 --> 00:01:16,420
wanna manage its DNS entries, its DNS

33
00:01:16,452 --> 00:01:16,999
records.

34
00:01:17,592 --> 00:01:20,013
To make sure that this domain actually

35
00:01:20,141 --> 00:01:22,834
routes to my VPS. Here I'm using Cloudflare.

36
00:01:23,106 --> 00:01:25,302
Obviously you can use any registrar of your

37
00:01:25,367 --> 00:01:27,931
choice. Here on Cloudflare, I do this by

38
00:01:28,476 --> 00:01:30,608
going to the DNS records for the domain I

39
00:01:30,656 --> 00:01:33,830
just acquired, click Add Record, add an A

40
00:01:33,862 --> 00:01:37,196
record, and then add for root so that really

41
00:01:37,212 --> 00:01:39,520
the root domain points at a certain

42
00:01:40,194 --> 00:01:44,201
IPv4 address. I grab my server IPv4 address,

43
00:01:45,389 --> 00:01:47,702
Add that here. I am fine with proxying

44
00:01:47,735 --> 00:01:49,582
traffic through Cloudflare. This is a

45
00:01:49,646 --> 00:01:51,365
Cloudflare-specific setting. It's not too

46
00:01:51,365 --> 00:01:53,759
important, but it will actually make sure

47
00:01:53,759 --> 00:01:56,120
that the Cloudflare CDN is used for

48
00:01:56,650 --> 00:01:59,430
caching responses and potentially speeding

49
00:01:59,606 --> 00:02:03,156
up responses by my website to end users, as

50
00:02:03,221 --> 00:02:05,727
well as protecting against DDoS attacks. But

51
00:02:05,952 --> 00:02:07,912
that's not too important. The important part

52
00:02:07,912 --> 00:02:10,257
is that we have an A record for our domain

53
00:02:10,386 --> 00:02:11,751
pointing at our IP.

54
00:02:12,314 --> 00:02:12,989
I save this

55
00:02:14,742 --> 00:02:17,348
and with that we know what our domain will

56
00:02:17,380 --> 00:02:18,618
be. So back here

57
00:02:19,374 --> 00:02:21,191
where I'm editing this.env file,

58
00:02:23,208 --> 00:02:25,319
I'll hook this up to vps-course

59
00:02:26,318 --> 00:02:28,413
and I'll also enter a valid email address

60
00:02:28,429 --> 00:02:29,847
which will be from another domain.

61
00:02:30,648 --> 00:02:31,812
It just needs to be valid.

62
00:02:32,490 --> 00:02:35,044
With that done, we can save this file and

63
00:02:35,076 --> 00:02:38,324
then as a next step, run docker-compose up

64
00:02:38,389 --> 00:02:39,569
-d --build,

65
00:02:40,236 --> 00:02:42,303
which will simply pick up our Docker Compose

66
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:42,544
file

67
00:02:43,121 --> 00:02:45,397
and create these 2 containers, the Traefik

68
00:02:45,525 --> 00:02:48,475
container and the app container, which

69
00:02:48,571 --> 00:02:49,869
contains the actual web app.

70
00:02:50,575 --> 00:02:53,252
By the way, here in those labels, in that

71
00:02:53,252 --> 00:02:55,736
metadata, which in the end configures how

72
00:02:56,041 --> 00:02:57,804
these containers are connected or how

73
00:02:57,804 --> 00:02:58,381
Traefik

74
00:02:58,926 --> 00:03:02,613
routes requests to our app server, you see

75
00:03:02,693 --> 00:03:05,130
that it's here where this app domain value

76
00:03:05,418 --> 00:03:08,408
we added to the.env file is injected and

77
00:03:08,699 --> 00:03:12,169
connects this certificate in the end to this

78
00:03:12,331 --> 00:03:12,508
app

79
00:03:13,057 --> 00:03:15,624
and where the email address is used, the

80
00:03:15,721 --> 00:03:16,593
Acme email address,

81
00:03:18,310 --> 00:03:20,675
which is also required for requesting that

82
00:03:20,675 --> 00:03:22,116
certificate. You don't need to change

83
00:03:22,116 --> 00:03:24,125
anything here. This is just how the things

84
00:03:24,222 --> 00:03:25,161
are linked up together.

85
00:03:26,515 --> 00:03:27,504
So docker-compose

86
00:03:28,168 --> 00:03:31,262
up it is. We can copy this command, execute

87
00:03:31,262 --> 00:03:33,741
it inside of our project folder on the VPS.

88
00:03:33,854 --> 00:03:34,405
That's important.

89
00:03:35,207 --> 00:03:37,519
And you may be getting a permission denied

90
00:03:38,017 --> 00:03:40,666
error here because we're on a user

91
00:03:41,309 --> 00:03:43,813
that doesn't have root privileges by

92
00:03:43,813 --> 00:03:46,623
default. Of course, we can run sudo docker

93
00:03:46,623 --> 00:03:49,337
composer up though to overcome this. So if

94
00:03:49,337 --> 00:03:50,477
we run this command,

95
00:03:51,119 --> 00:03:53,029
you see now it works and now it's

96
00:03:53,029 --> 00:03:54,876
downloading that Traefik image, which I

97
00:03:54,876 --> 00:03:56,787
mentioned would be a prebuilt image.

98
00:03:57,477 --> 00:03:59,131
It's building our own image based on the

99
00:03:59,147 --> 00:04:01,395
Dockerfile we shared, that was part of the

100
00:04:01,395 --> 00:04:03,369
project. That's what it's currently doing

101
00:04:03,401 --> 00:04:03,626
here.

102
00:04:04,284 --> 00:04:05,761
And that can take a short while,

103
00:04:06,436 --> 00:04:08,458
but in the end it should be done, should

104
00:04:08,458 --> 00:04:09,662
finish without an error.

105
00:04:10,529 --> 00:04:11,878
And you can thereafter

106
00:04:12,857 --> 00:04:14,254
run sudo docker

107
00:04:15,442 --> 00:04:15,875
ps

108
00:04:16,726 --> 00:04:19,263
and that will list running containers. And

109
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:21,912
it's a bit hard to see here since I'm zoomed

110
00:04:22,008 --> 00:04:23,726
in quite a bit, but we got 2

111
00:04:24,352 --> 00:04:26,760
container IDs. We got 2 containers up and

112
00:04:26,760 --> 00:04:28,815
running, the Traefik container and our

113
00:04:28,992 --> 00:04:30,035
actual app container.

114
00:04:31,253 --> 00:04:33,449
Now with those containers up and running,

115
00:04:33,658 --> 00:04:35,517
we're not entirely there yet though.

116
00:04:36,767 --> 00:04:39,091
Our containers, specifically the traffic

117
00:04:39,091 --> 00:04:41,736
container, listen for traffic on certain

118
00:04:41,736 --> 00:04:42,233
ports,

119
00:04:42,890 --> 00:04:47,554
specifically on ports 80 and 443. 443 for

120
00:04:47,762 --> 00:04:47,842
the

121
00:04:48,403 --> 00:04:50,102
encrypted HTTPS traffic.

122
00:04:50,631 --> 00:04:53,292
80 also must be kept open even though that

123
00:04:53,292 --> 00:04:55,472
is for unsecured traffic because whilst this

124
00:04:55,536 --> 00:04:57,475
will not be used to actually access our

125
00:04:57,620 --> 00:05:00,906
website, will do that via 443.

126
00:05:01,707 --> 00:05:03,951
Whilst it will not be used for that, it must

127
00:05:03,951 --> 00:05:06,981
be open for Let's Encrypt to be able to

128
00:05:06,997 --> 00:05:09,947
verify that we own the domain we told it the

129
00:05:09,947 --> 00:05:13,089
certificate is for. They will send a request

130
00:05:13,089 --> 00:05:13,602
to this

131
00:05:14,515 --> 00:05:16,423
port here in the end and look for a certain

132
00:05:16,439 --> 00:05:19,148
file that is created by Traefik, so we don't

133
00:05:19,148 --> 00:05:21,184
need to worry about that. But the port must

134
00:05:21,184 --> 00:05:23,508
be open so that Let's Encrypt can verify

135
00:05:23,749 --> 00:05:26,281
that we own the domain, and only then will

136
00:05:26,313 --> 00:05:28,766
they issue the certificate. That's why both

137
00:05:29,023 --> 00:05:30,850
80 and 443 must be open. Open

138
00:05:31,748 --> 00:05:34,216
and our compose.yaml file, which configures

139
00:05:34,313 --> 00:05:36,653
how the Docker containers are started, takes

140
00:05:36,653 --> 00:05:37,390
care about that.

141
00:05:37,919 --> 00:05:40,308
For the Traefik service, the Traefik reverse

142
00:05:40,420 --> 00:05:43,274
proxy, this entry here ensures that these

143
00:05:43,450 --> 00:05:46,800
ports are in the end open and forwarded into

144
00:05:46,833 --> 00:05:48,211
the container. But

145
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:50,407
this has one important implication.

146
00:05:51,081 --> 00:05:53,758
We also must make sure that the firewall

147
00:05:53,790 --> 00:05:57,108
that protects our VPS allows traffic through

148
00:05:57,156 --> 00:05:59,048
these ports. Otherwise, this is all

149
00:05:59,401 --> 00:06:01,646
pointless and worthless. It's nice that we

150
00:06:01,742 --> 00:06:04,067
listen for traffic on these ports. If our

151
00:06:04,067 --> 00:06:07,114
firewall blocks access to these ports,

152
00:06:07,739 --> 00:06:10,096
there is no way for traffic to reach our

153
00:06:10,096 --> 00:06:12,854
server, neither for the Let's Encrypt

154
00:06:13,271 --> 00:06:16,510
verification request nor for the regular web

155
00:06:16,558 --> 00:06:17,792
traffic we wanna handle.

156
00:06:18,979 --> 00:06:21,256
And therefore, we wanna open up these ports.

157
00:06:21,544 --> 00:06:23,757
Now, if you're using UFW, which I mentioned

158
00:06:23,869 --> 00:06:24,254
earlier,

159
00:06:25,136 --> 00:06:28,089
You could add rules that open up these

160
00:06:28,202 --> 00:06:30,882
ports, but there actually is an issue here.

161
00:06:31,732 --> 00:06:34,926
When using Docker, they themselves mention

162
00:06:34,926 --> 00:06:37,189
in their docs that if you're using UFW

163
00:06:38,329 --> 00:06:40,704
to manage firewall settings, you must be

164
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:40,993
aware

165
00:06:41,603 --> 00:06:43,657
that if you expose container ports using

166
00:06:43,769 --> 00:06:46,770
Docker, these ports bypass your firewall

167
00:06:46,819 --> 00:06:50,285
rules simply due to how Docker and UFW work

168
00:06:50,333 --> 00:06:50,879
internally.

169
00:06:51,649 --> 00:06:53,367
So put in other words, you don't really need

170
00:06:53,399 --> 00:06:56,897
to open those ports with UFW because using

171
00:06:56,930 --> 00:06:58,823
Docker already does that.

172
00:06:59,497 --> 00:07:02,210
In my case here, however, I'm using the

173
00:07:02,210 --> 00:07:04,842
Hetzner firewall though. And there I have

174
00:07:04,874 --> 00:07:07,265
not opened up these ports yet. And that

175
00:07:07,265 --> 00:07:10,138
firewall sits in front of my VPS, is not

176
00:07:10,234 --> 00:07:12,497
overwritten by Docker, and therefore must be

177
00:07:12,497 --> 00:07:15,306
adjusted. And if you are using Hetzner or

178
00:07:15,306 --> 00:07:16,814
some other provider that has a

179
00:07:16,814 --> 00:07:20,040
provider-specific firewall, you must do that

180
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:20,185
too.

181
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:23,807
So you wanna allow traffic from any IPv4 and

182
00:07:23,887 --> 00:07:26,222
IPv6 address via TCP

183
00:07:26,801 --> 00:07:27,494
to port 80.

184
00:07:29,281 --> 00:07:32,810
And you wanna do the same for port 443 for

185
00:07:32,924 --> 00:07:33,475
HTTPS.

186
00:07:34,221 --> 00:07:35,583
You have to save these changes.

187
00:07:37,402 --> 00:07:40,230
And on the VPS, you can actually disable UFW

188
00:07:40,471 --> 00:07:42,897
if you are using such an extra firewall.

189
00:07:43,668 --> 00:07:47,049
The problem is that of course I did start my

190
00:07:47,049 --> 00:07:48,629
Docker container before I

191
00:07:49,188 --> 00:07:50,077
opened up my ports. So in case you did that

192
00:07:50,126 --> 00:07:50,225
too,

193
00:07:52,106 --> 00:07:54,820
you can restart it with sudo docker compose

194
00:07:55,719 --> 00:07:58,240
restart traffic. Traffic is the name of that

195
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:00,279
specific service I wanna restart. And if you

196
00:08:00,311 --> 00:08:02,158
run that, it does just what the name implies

197
00:08:02,158 --> 00:08:05,048
and that should trigger a new certificate

198
00:08:05,449 --> 00:08:08,002
request and that should then work since we

199
00:08:08,002 --> 00:08:09,046
did open up that port.

200
00:08:09,688 --> 00:08:11,567
And with that done, you should be able to

201
00:08:11,567 --> 00:08:12,001
visit

202
00:08:12,579 --> 00:08:14,794
your domain, in my case, vpscourse.com

203
00:08:15,790 --> 00:08:16,898
and see your site.

204
00:08:18,296 --> 00:08:20,452
And I should also be able to submit an idea.

205
00:08:21,014 --> 00:08:21,722
And that works.

206
00:08:23,202 --> 00:08:25,389
And this is how you can deploy a web

207
00:08:25,389 --> 00:08:28,880
application with Docker with HTTPS support

208
00:08:29,459 --> 00:08:30,761
onto a VPS.
